|> Exercise
    work=ing
    handle=_unit_dot_product_geometric_interpretation_

    !! Using a geometric argument, 
    Show that
    $$
    \cos(\alpha - \beta)
    $$
    equals
    $$
    \cos\alpha\cos\beta + \sin\alpha\sin\beta
    $$
    for all $\alpha$, $\beta \in \rr$
    via a geometric argument.
    !! by a geometric argument. 
    (I.e., AVOID using the angle-sum formula.)

    |> Solution

        !! It is easy to see that
        We know (or should know, or are about to
        know) (or are about to _believe_) that

        $$
        \cos(\alpha - \beta)
        $$

        !! equals 
        is the projection of a unit vector of angle
        $\alpha$ onto a ruler of angle $\beta$:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_unit_dot_product_sol1_v4.svg

        In what follows we will write ‘$\vecb{i}$’
        and ‘$\vecb{j}$’, respectively, for the vectors
        $(1, 0)$ and $(0, 1)$ (in the manner of physicists):

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_unit_dot_product_sol2.svg

        As established in Exercise >>_project_i_and_j_
        $\vecb{i}$ and $\vecb{j}$ project to $\cos\beta$,
        $\sin\beta$ respectively on the ruler: 

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_unit_dot_product_sol3.svg

        On the other hand, the vector $(\cos\alpha, \sin\alpha)$ is
        the sum of 
        a scaled copy of $\vecb{i}$ and scaled copy
        of $\vecb{j}$, 
        specifically the sum of $\vecb{i}\cdot\cos(\alpha)$ and $\vecb{j}\cdot\sin(\alpha)$:
        !! with the scaling coefficients being
        !! $\cos(\alpha)$, $\sin(\alpha)$:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_unit_dot_product_sol5.svg

        The scaled copy of $\vecb{i}$ projects to
        $\cos(\alpha)\cdot\cos(\beta)$ on the ruler, 
        by proportionality:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_unit_dot_product_sol6.svg

        !! a
        !! projection-of-the-scalar-multiple will come
        !! out the same as the scalar-multiple-of-the-projection:
        !!   $\vecb{i}$

        Likewise, the scaled copy of $\vecb{j}$ projects
        to $\sin(\alpha)\cdot\sin(\beta)$:
        !! Likewise, 
        !! $\vecb{j}\cdot\sin(\alpha)$
        !! projects
        !! to $\sin(\alpha)\cdot\sin(\beta)$: 
        !! on the ruler, because
        !! here the scaling factor by which $\vecb{j}$ is scaled
        !! is $\sin(\alpha)$:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_unit_dot_product_sol7.svg

        However the sum-of-projections is the
        projection-of-the-sum...
        !! However, treating the 
        !! projections of $\vecb{i}\cdot\cos(\alpha)$
        !! and $\vecb{j}\cdot\sin(\alpha)$
        !! as displacements on the ruler, and adding these
        !! displacements together, we obtain the
        !! projection of $(\cos\alpha, \sin\alpha)$:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_unit_dot_product_sol8_v3.svg

        !! Thus,
        ...giving us...

        |> Boxed
            $$
            \cos(\alpha-\beta) = \cos\alpha\cos\beta + \sin\alpha\sin\beta
            $$

        ...since we always knew, from the beginning, that the projection of
        $(\cos \alpha, \sin \alpha)$ onto the ruler of angle $\beta$ was (is)
        $\cos(\alpha - \beta)$!
